course: history
lesson: 43
title: Lithuanian and Polish times, the first Cossacks
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: After the Mongols, most Ukrainian lands came under the Grand Duchy of Lithuania; Danylo calls it «really a Ukrainian state» and the teacher calls it «Lithuanian occupation», but the truth sits between: the rulers were Lithuanian while Ruthenian, the ancestor of Ukrainian and Belarusian, was the language of its documents.
explain: In 1569 the Union of Lublin put most Ukrainian lands under the Polish crown; it was peaceful for the nobles but hard for many peasants, like a new school rule that suits the prefects and burdens everyone else.
explain: These centuries also brought schools and books: the Ostroh Bible of 1581, the Kyiv-Mohyla Academy of 1632, and towns running their own courts under Magdeburg rights; the teacher had not heard of Ostroh, and Danylo was right about it.
explain: On the wild steppe frontier the first Cossacks appeared, runaway peasants and others who fished, hunted and fought Tatar slave raiders; they loved freedom, and many also served the Polish king for pay, so both «free» and «paid» are true.
explain: The wise lesson: one treaty can be good for some and bad for others, and past wrongs, like the slave raids, are history to learn, not a reason to hate a people living today.
fact: The Grand Duchy of Lithuania took most Ukrainian lands in the 14th century; its rulers were Lithuanian, its official written language was largely Ruthenian, and Orthodox Rus nobles kept their rights for a time.
fact: In 1569 the Union of Lublin joined Poland and Lithuania into the Commonwealth, and most Ukrainian lands passed to the Polish crown; many local nobles agreed for their own rights, while Orthodox peasants later faced heavier serfdom (being tied to a landowner's land and made to work for him).
fact: Prince Kostiantyn Ostrozky founded the Ostroh Academy around 1576, and the Ostroh Bible of 1581, printed by Ivan Fedorov, was the first complete Bible in Church Slavonic; Krakow's university (1364) was older.
fact: The Kyiv-Mohyla Academy was founded in 1632 by Metropolitan Petro Mohyla, a senior bishop, and became a leading school of the Orthodox world; many of its graduates went on to teach and lead the church in Moscow and St Petersburg.
fact: Many towns in Ukrainian lands, such as Lviv and later Kyiv, received Magdeburg rights, which let townspeople run their own courts and councils, though Orthodox residents were sometimes kept out.
fact: In 1596, at the Union of Brest, some Orthodox bishops accepted the Pope while keeping their Eastern rites (their old ways of holding services), creating the Greek Catholic Church, strong in western Ukraine today; others refused, and both churches are Ukrainian.
fact: Cossacks appeared in the 15th–16th centuries in the steppe borderlands; they fished, hunted and fought Tatar raiders, and many served the Polish king as registered Cossacks for pay.
fact: Crimean Tatar raids took many captives from Ukrainian, Polish and Russian lands to the slave markets between the 15th and 18th centuries; historians' estimates range from hundreds of thousands to over a million, and because few records survive, the numbers are uncertain.
fact: Roksolana (Hurrem Sultan), a captive probably from Rohatyn in western Ukraine, became the wife of Sultan Suleiman the Magnificent and had great influence at court, but she did not rule the empire.
fact: Prince Dmytro Vyshnevetsky, called Baida, built a fortress on the island of Mala Khortytsia around 1552–1556, often seen as a forerunner of the Zaporizhian Sich; the story of his death in Istanbul comes from a folk song.
fact: In 1621 at Khotyn a Polish-Lithuanian army with about 40,000 Cossacks under Hetman Petro Sahaidachny (a hetman was the Cossacks' chosen commander) held off a large Ottoman army; the Cossacks were crucial, but the army was a joint one.
exercise: Which state took most Ukrainian lands in the 14th century? | The Grand Duchy of Lithuania | The Ottoman Empire of the sultans | The Kingdom of Sweden | The Russian Empire
exercise: What was the main written language of the Grand Duchy's documents? | Ruthenian, an ancestor of Ukrainian and Belarusian | Modern Russian, the same as is spoken in Moscow today | Lithuanian only | Latin and English
exercise: What did the Union of Lublin create in 1569? | The Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth | The Zaporizhian Sich below the rapids | The Russian Empire ruled by the tsars | The Golden Horde of the Mongol khans
exercise: Who found the Union of Lublin harder over time? | Many Orthodox peasants, who faced heavier serfdom | The Polish king, who lost his crown to the Lithuanian nobles | Nobody, it helped everyone equally | Only the Mongols
exercise: Who printed the Ostroh Bible in 1581? | Ivan Fedorov | Taras Shevchenko | Metropolitan Petro Mohyla | Yaroslav the Wise
exercise: Which university is older than the Ostroh Academy? | Krakow, founded in 1364 | Kyiv-Mohyla, founded in 1632 | Lviv Polytechnic, founded in 1844 | None, Ostroh was the first in the world
exercise: Who founded the Kyiv-Mohyla Academy in 1632? | Metropolitan Petro Mohyla | Princess Olha, Ihor's widow | Empress Catherine II of Russia | Hetman Bohdan Khmelnytsky
exercise: Where did many Kyiv-Mohyla graduates go to work? | Moscow and St Petersburg, in the church and schools | Paris, as teachers to the sons and daughters of the French kings | Istanbul, as sultans | Canada, as farmers
exercise: What could a town with Magdeburg rights do? | Run its own courts and council | Print its own Bible only | Choose its own king from among the townspeople | Stop paying any taxes forever
exercise: What did the Greek Catholic Church keep from Eastern tradition? | Its Eastern rites | Nothing, it became fully Latin | The old Mongol laws of the Horde | The rule of the hetman
exercise: In which year was the Union of Brest? | 1596 | 1569 | 1654 | 988
exercise: Who were the first Cossacks? | Runaway peasants and others on the steppe frontier | Knights sent from France by the king to guard the steppe for Poland | Vikings from Sweden | Monks from Constantinople
exercise: What were registered Cossacks? | Cossacks paid to serve the Polish king | Cossacks who wrote down all the laws of the Sich | Cossacks who never fought | Cossacks who lived in Moscow
exercise: Why are numbers of captives from the Tatar raids uncertain? | Records are few and incomplete, so historians estimate | Every captive was counted exactly and written in a big book in Istanbul | The raids never took captives | The numbers were kept secret until 2022
exercise: Who was Roksolana? | A captive from Ukraine who became Suleiman's influential wife | The ruler of the whole Ottoman Empire, who sat on the throne after Suleiman died | A Cossack hetman | The first Queen of France
exercise: What did Dmytro Vyshnevetsky build around 1552–1556? | A fortress on Mala Khortytsia | Saint Sophia Cathedral in Kyiv | The Ostroh Academy and its press | The port city of Odesa on the sea
exercise: Who led about 40,000 Cossacks at Khotyn in 1621? | Petro Sahaidachny | Hetman Ivan Mazepa | Nestor Makhno of Huliaipole | Prince Sviatoslav of Kyiv
test: Teacher says «Lithuanian occupation», Danylo says «it was a Ukrainian state». Which is fairest? | A shared state with Lithuanian rulers where Ruthenian culture was very strong | Danylo's right, the rulers were Ukrainians | The teacher's right, Ruthenian was banned in all the courts, churches and schools of the Duchy | It was a Mongol state
test: Danylo says the Poles grabbed Ukraine by a trick in 1569, teacher says «a peaceful union». Wise answer? | Peaceful for the nobles, who agreed, but hard for many peasants | Only a trick, nobody agreed | Only good for everyone | It never happened
test: Danylo says Ostroh was the first uni in Eastern Europe, teacher never heard of it. What's honest? | An early, important school, though Krakow's university was older | The very first university in the whole world, older than Bologna, Paris or Oxford | It never existed | It was in Moscow
test: Danylo says Kyiv-Mohyla taught all Russia to read. What's fair? | Too much, but its influence on Russian schools and the church was huge | Exactly true, nobody in Russia could read a single word before it opened | It had no influence at all | It taught only Latin to Swedes
test: A Lviv shoemaker in 1600 has a quarrel with a neighbour. Where might it be judged? | In the town court, thanks to Magdeburg rights | Only by the sultan in Istanbul | By the Pope in person | Nowhere, there were no courts
test: Danylo's family is Orthodox and Oksana's is Greek Catholic. Danylo says hers was forced on Ukrainians. What's wise? | It began in a split among Ukrainians, and both churches are Ukrainian | Only Orthodox is Ukrainian | Greek Catholics are Polish, not Ukrainian, because the Pope sent Polish priests | Both churches came from the Mongols
test: Danylo says Cossacks were free and never served anyone. The teacher lists the registered Cossacks. Who's right? | Both: they valued freedom and also made paid deals with kings | Only Danylo | Only the teacher, they had no freedom | Neither, Cossacks never existed
test: someone says crimean tatars today should pay for the old raids. what's the wise answer? | Past wrongs are history, not a reason to hate a people living now | Yes, every Tatar alive today is guilty and should pay for what happened long ago | The raids never happened | Only the raids matter, not later history
test: Danylo says a Ukrainian girl ruled the Ottoman Empire. What's the right word? | Influential: she had great power at court but did not rule | Sultan: she ruled alone | Nothing: she was never there | Hetman: she led the Cossacks
test: A folk song says Baida died in Istanbul. A fortress on Mala Khortytsia was really built. What's the lesson? | A folk song can be built around a real man and real places | A song is the same as a chronicle | The fortress must be a legend too | Folk songs are always exactly true
test: Danylo: «Cossacks alone saved Europe at Khotyn». Teacher: «the Polish army won». Fair answer? | They fought together, and Sahaidachny's Cossacks were crucial | Only the Cossacks fought | Only the Poles fought | The Ottomans won easily
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the linguistic arrangements of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania? | Its official documents were composed largely in Ruthenian | Its documents were composed exclusively in modern Russian | Writing was prohibited throughout the Duchy | Its documents were composed in Mongolian
exam: Identify the consequence of the Union of Lublin of fifteen hundred and sixty-nine in respect of the Ukrainian lands. | The greater part thereof passed to the Polish crown | They were ceded to the Ottoman Empire by treaty | They became an independent republic under the Cossacks | They were annexed by Sweden
exam: Ascertain the ecclesiastical outcome of the Union of Brest of fifteen hundred and ninety-six. | The formation of a church in communion with the Pope retaining Eastern rites | The abolition of all churches in Ukraine | The conversion of Kyiv to Islam | The founding of the Kyiv-Mohyla Academy
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the battle of Khotyn in sixteen hundred and twenty-one. | A joint Polish-Lithuanian and Cossack force withstood a large Ottoman army | The Cossacks alone defeated the whole of Europe | The Ottoman army captured Krakow | No Cossacks were present
